            "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
            "body": "<p>As an Indian citizen you need a transit visa for Germany and the Frankfurt airport operates from 5 AM to 11 PM check Frankfurt airport website.</p>\n<p>See this note source here <a href=\"https://www.germany.info/blob/917514/d7511f4c7c8abcadbf209968aaea36bb/airport-transit-country-list-data.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Germany Transit Visa Conditions </a></p>\n<p>The document first states</p>\n<p>The vast majority of foreign travelers benefit from the \u201ctransit privilege\u201d - if during a stopover at a\nGerman airport, you do not leave the International Airport Area and if the destination is not in a Schengen country, you do not need a transit visa.</p>\n<p>\u2026 conditions and then it says</p>\n<p>Nationals of the following countries do not have the transit privilege and therefore require an airport transit visa (category A) for transit at a German airport</p>\n<p>Unfortunately India is one of them.</p>\n<p>Then the document outlines the exhaustive list of exception too but as I see you have a PR in Canada and the exception does not state Canada either</p>\n<p>Then this another source that I found says Canada <a href=\"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/visa-service/buergerservice/faq/33-transit/606710\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">2</a></p>\n<p>Finally the German Embassy in Canada website<a href=\"https://canada.diplo.de/ca-en/consular-services/visa/-/2169884\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">3</a>states you don\u2019t need a transit visa if you are a permanent resident.</p>\n<p>So there you go with Indian citizen and Canadian PR you don\u2019t need a transit visa but  you will have to   stay in the International area.</p>\n"
